            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we found some substantial air leakage in the following locations:



** The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch once you finish topping up the insulation. 



** Around the wood stoves combustion air pipe where is connects to the exterior wall.



** Around ceiling penetrations, such as light fixtures.



** The wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls, as well as the frame around the back exterior door is allowing air leakage &amp; heat loss. There is also air leakage along the header spaces where outside electrical outlets are located.



Savings: For a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, you could potentially save $ 100 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.9 GJ, and qualify for a $500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 3.5 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. You mentioned of plans to renovate, removing the batt insulation &amp; installing a minimum R22 spray ployurethane foam insulation. This would require filling the full 3.5 inch cavity of the 2x4 wall studs. To create an easier install, and less potential for penetrations into the new spray foam, I recommend strapping the wall before hanging the new drywall. 



Savings:

By increasing your walls by a minimum R10, you could potentially save $ 260 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation.  I have modelled adding an additional 11.5 inches (R39) of blown cellulose insulation, for a total insulation value of R51.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R51, you could potentially save $ 710 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 20.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).

As you mentioned you may potentially reuse the inulation from the wall cavities in attic, the re-used insulation would not qualify under the rebate, however if any additional insulation is purchased for insulating the attic, this would qualify. 





To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilings>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

I recommend you install an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92.You could potentially save $ 1,050 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program </HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1,350 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 30.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>COMBUSTION AIR



I did not see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the furnace.

I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your furnace is satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when thefurnace is operating. 



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $0.40 per kWh for off-grid generators

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

